# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# `go build .` at the root, and where the release workflow stages its archives.
/surmise
/dist/

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# The workspace is committed on purpose: it points the build at the patched
# bubbletea copy under third_party. See go.work for why that is not a `replace`.

# env file. The glob covers .env.local too, which `vercel link` writes to hold
# an OIDC token — nothing in the build reads it, but it must never be committed.
.env*

# Browser build output. Ignored, not merely untidy: release.yml relies on the
# worktree staying clean, because a dirty tree makes Go stamp every release
# binary "dirty", and ignored files are not reported as changes.
/web/dist/
/web/node_modules/

# `vercel link` wrote the project ids here when the site was on Vercel. Kept
# until that project is deleted, so a local copy is never committed.
/.vercel/

# wrangler's local state, and an install of the deploy CLI if one is ever run
# in place. Same reason as above: release.yml needs a clean tree.
/worker/node_modules/
/worker/.wrangler/
# Generated by `wrangler types` from wrangler.jsonc on every check.
/worker/worker-configuration.d.ts

# Local scratch
notes/
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
.claude/

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/
